; Tutorial_4_Read_the_Workbook_and_Screenshots.inix -- read the workbook and screenshots

[about]
Title=4 - Read the Workbook and Screenshots
Intro=The workbook urlCheck writes for each page: its sheets, its screenshot links, and the other files in the page's folder.
Setup=A page checked in walk three, and Excel installed.
Homework=Open one violation's screenshot from the workbook and share it with a sighted colleague.

[step]

Say=One want: the same results in a form I can sort and share with a developer, with a picture of each problem for sighted colleagues.
Key=

[step]

Say=In the page's folder, report dot x l s x is the workbook. I open it in Excel.
Key=Enter
Hear=report dot x l s x - Excel
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=It has a sheet for each kind of result: violations, passes, incomplete, and rules that did not apply.
Key=

[step]

Say=Control plus Page Down moves from sheet to sheet, and the screen reader says each sheet's name.
Key=Control+Page Down
Hear=Passes
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Control plus Page Up returns to the violations.
Key=Control+Page Up
Hear=Violations
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Each row is one rule and one element, with columns for the rule, its impact, the WCAG criterion, the element and the fix.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=image-alt, critical, 1.1.1
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Sorting by impact, most critical first, puts the worst problems at the top for a developer's list.
Key=

[step]

Say=The Results sheet has an Image column: each cell is a link to a screenshot of that one element.
Key=
Hear=Image, violations backslash image-001 dot p n g, link
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Control plus K, or Enter on the link, opens the picture, which a sighted colleague can see at once.
Key=

[step]

Say=The screenshots are in the page's violations folder, numbered in order, image 001, image 002 and so on.
Key=

[step]

Say=The links are relative, so the whole page folder can be zipped, emailed or moved, and they still work wherever it lands.
Key=

[step]

Say=When axe's description of an element cannot be found on the page, such as an element hidden or inside a shadow tree, no screenshot is taken, and the row says nothing is attached.
Key=

[step]

Say=Besides the workbook, the folder holds page dot p n g, a picture of the whole page, and page dot h t m, its source as it was checked.
Key=

[step]

Say=Results dot j s o n holds everything in a form other programs read, for anyone building on urlCheck's results.
Key=

[step]

Say=And page dot y a m l is the page's accessibility tree, roles and names as a screen reader receives them, readable in any text editor.
Key=

[step]

Say=Reading that tree is a quick way to hear how a page is built, without the page's visual layout in the way.
Key=

[step]

Say=Excel's AutoFilter narrows the sheet to one impact or one rule, which turns a long list into a short one.
Key=Alt+Down Arrow
Hear=Filter, Impact
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Each sheet has its header row in the first row, so the screen reader announces the column name with every cell.
Key=

[step]

Say=The Passes sheet is worth a look: it shows the rules a page already meets, which is good news to share alongside the problems.
Key=

[step]

Say=A developer can be sent the workbook alone; its screenshot links need the violations folder beside it, so zip the page's whole folder.
Key=

[step]

Say=A report to read, a workbook to sort, a picture of each problem: one folder serves blind and sighted reviewers alike. The next walk checks a list of pages.
Key=
